8
00:00:49,300 --> 00:00:55,100
When we're talking about communication, so you talked about the keys.

9
00:00:55,100 --> 00:00:58,580
Oh, I lose my keys too or I forget things too.

10
00:00:58,580 --> 00:01:01,120
That's a very unhelpful thing.

11
00:01:01,120 --> 00:01:05,200
How can we help people realize what they're doing and then help them, give them something

12
00:01:05,200 --> 00:01:06,200
to do or something?

13
00:01:06,200 --> 00:01:10,920
Because I know it's only so helpful to tell them this isn't helpful if we don't give them

14
00:01:10,920 --> 00:01:18,480
something to try differently, then they'll just be like, well, what else can I do?

15
00:01:18,480 --> 00:01:23,900
What are you thinking of that we could do that would be helpful for people who have

16
00:01:23,900 --> 00:01:24,900
a lot of experience?

17
00:01:24,900 --> 00:01:28,140
Like five to 10 minutes to watch.

18
00:01:28,140 --> 00:01:34,980
Well, I think you and I have talked about the whack-a-mole and I don't know if we were

19
00:01:34,980 --> 00:01:35,980
going to talk about that.

20
00:01:35,980 --> 00:01:36,980
You want to.

21
00:01:36,980 --> 00:01:45,460
Well, that was another one of the things that I knew maybe what to do comes out of what

22
00:01:45,460 --> 00:01:46,460
not to do.

23
00:01:46,460 --> 00:01:52,920
The whack-a-mole approach of what we're talking about communication.

24
00:01:52,920 --> 00:01:53,920
Yeah.

25
00:01:53,920 --> 00:01:54,900
Yeah.

26
00:01:54,900 --> 00:01:58,920
I think that's a really good question on trying to figure out how to help the people living

27
00:01:58,920 --> 00:01:59,920
with dementia.

28
00:01:59,920 --> 00:02:02,600
It's throwing out, is it this?

29
00:02:02,600 --> 00:02:03,600
Is it that?

30
00:02:03,600 --> 00:02:04,600
Do you want this?

31
00:02:04,600 --> 00:02:05,600
Do you want that?

32
00:02:05,600 --> 00:02:10,360
And every time there's noise coming out of the other person's mouth, my brain can't do

33
00:02:10,360 --> 00:02:11,500
more than one thing.

34
00:02:11,500 --> 00:02:13,660
So it's listening to you ask me, is it this?

35
00:02:13,660 --> 00:02:14,660
Is it that?

36
00:02:14,660 --> 00:02:19,980
Rather than letting me process and figure it out.

37
00:02:19,980 --> 00:02:24,780
So, Joanna, are you really trying to think about the whack-a-mole and how it's really

38
00:02:24,780 --> 00:02:25,780
supposed to go?

39
00:02:25,780 --> 00:02:28,720
Because if everybody's giving you things to think about, then it really shut you down.

40
00:02:28,720 --> 00:02:31,620
But if it really shuts you down, but really, see, I thought I was offering lots of different

41
00:02:31,620 --> 00:02:32,620
things for you to do.

42
00:02:32,620 --> 00:02:33,620
Isn't that really how it makes sense?

43
00:02:33,620 --> 00:02:35,920
I mean, you're really following everything I'm saying, right?

44
00:02:35,920 --> 00:02:43,100
I got, hey, Joanna, I got that part.

45
00:02:43,100 --> 00:02:48,780
And then there was a whole bunch of words after that, but I don't know really what it

46
00:02:48,780 --> 00:02:51,860
was all meaning exactly.

47
00:02:51,860 --> 00:02:53,740
And I think that when...

48
00:02:53,740 --> 00:02:54,700
So, yeah.

49
00:02:54,780 --> 00:02:55,780
Exactly.

50
00:02:55,780 --> 00:03:01,160
So I used to tell when I had employees way back in the day, I'd say, I want you to come

51
00:03:01,160 --> 00:03:05,280
to me with any problems, but not without thinking of a solution first.

52
00:03:05,280 --> 00:03:07,660
Now, a solution may not work.

53
00:03:07,660 --> 00:03:09,940
We'll discuss it.

54
00:03:09,940 --> 00:03:12,760
But I cannot stand to hear people complain.

55
00:03:12,760 --> 00:03:17,200
And now I feel like I'm one of those people that complains all the time, when really what

56
00:03:17,200 --> 00:03:19,920
I'm trying to do is problem solve.

57
00:03:19,920 --> 00:03:22,380
I'm trying to find a solution.

58
00:03:22,380 --> 00:03:24,740
So I have to talk through it.

59
00:03:24,740 --> 00:03:31,560
Sometimes, well, I can't do that if someone's going, they're trying to help me.

60
00:03:31,560 --> 00:03:38,740
They're trying to help me with words and with even the audible, uh-huh, uh-huh, I know what

61
00:03:38,740 --> 00:03:39,740
you mean.

62
00:03:39,740 --> 00:03:41,740
Yeah, uh-huh, I hear you.

63
00:03:41,740 --> 00:03:42,740
Yes.

64
00:03:42,740 --> 00:03:50,740
This whole, I think it's like this counseling approach, which I don't know if all counselors

65
00:03:50,740 --> 00:03:51,740
do that.

66
00:03:51,740 --> 00:03:52,740
I don't think they do.

67
00:03:52,740 --> 00:03:53,740
I don't think they'd be in business.

68
00:03:53,740 --> 00:03:54,740
But that's...

69
00:03:54,740 --> 00:03:55,740
That's just a...

70
00:03:55,740 --> 00:04:01,180
I can't get anywhere with that, because every time you hear the uh-huh, uh-huh, I have to

71
00:04:01,180 --> 00:04:05,180
go back to the beginning of my thought and my story.

72
00:04:05,180 --> 00:04:13,060
So unless they want to hear once upon a time, hundreds of times before we get to the big

73
00:04:13,060 --> 00:04:17,260
bad wolf and, you know, then...

74
00:04:17,260 --> 00:04:19,060
And it's so hard in our culture.

75
00:04:19,060 --> 00:04:23,860
Again, I feel like I'm complaining, but what I'm trying to explain is we're in a culture,

76
00:04:23,860 --> 00:04:24,700
where...

77
00:04:24,700 --> 00:04:31,580
We don't allow for a lot of silence and a lot of patience, a lot of dead air.

78
00:04:31,580 --> 00:04:33,020
That's supposed to be a bad thing.

79
00:04:33,020 --> 00:04:38,140
We're supposed to fill it with noise.

80
00:04:38,140 --> 00:04:39,140
And that...

81
00:04:39,140 --> 00:04:42,420
That doesn't work.

82
00:04:42,420 --> 00:04:51,140
That doesn't work when a person living with dementia is already working from the moment

83
00:04:51,140 --> 00:04:52,700
they open their eyes in the morning.

84
00:04:52,700 --> 00:04:53,700
Nothing I do is inbound.

85
00:04:53,700 --> 00:04:54,700
I'm not...

86
00:04:54,700 --> 00:04:55,700
I'm not involuntary anymore.

87
00:04:55,700 --> 00:05:04,140
I'm thinking about everything I'm doing, from getting up and walking to the bathroom and

88
00:05:04,140 --> 00:05:08,300
getting a glass of water.

89
00:05:08,300 --> 00:05:16,380
I'm having to think about not putting the hot water in the glass to take my medication,

90
00:05:16,380 --> 00:05:19,860
to put the cold water in, which side that's on.

91
00:05:19,860 --> 00:05:20,860
That's...

92
00:05:20,860 --> 00:05:22,700
That's used to be involuntary.

93
00:05:22,700 --> 00:05:24,580
Does that make sense?

94
00:05:24,700 --> 00:05:25,900
You're using brain power.

95
00:05:25,900 --> 00:05:27,820
Now I'm having to think about that.

96
00:05:27,820 --> 00:05:29,820
So it's work.

97
00:05:32,460 --> 00:05:39,180
So if you can imagine all those things throughout your day that are automatic or involuntary,

98
00:05:39,980 --> 00:05:42,860
that's work for someone like me.

99
00:05:44,700 --> 00:05:45,420
And so I'm...

100
00:05:46,460 --> 00:05:48,380
I get tired really fast.

101
00:05:49,820 --> 00:05:54,460
And I think that the easiest thing and the most successful thing

102
00:05:54,700 --> 00:06:06,460
for caregivers to do that's going to be successful for everybody and to me easier is to slow

103
00:06:06,460 --> 00:06:10,660
down and to be patient.

104
00:06:10,660 --> 00:06:15,860
And I don't know how that works in a residential setting, you know, in a long-term care setting

105
00:06:15,860 --> 00:06:20,820
where there's staff because, you know, you got to get through so many...

106
00:06:20,820 --> 00:06:22,380
I don't know what that looks like.

107
00:06:22,380 --> 00:06:23,700
That's not my environment.

108
00:06:23,700 --> 00:06:26,700
All I know is what works and what doesn't.

109
00:06:26,700 --> 00:06:33,700
And what we see the masses doing for the most part out there is what doesn't work.

110
00:06:33,700 --> 00:06:41,700
And I think if they could start doing, implementing this,

111
00:06:41,700 --> 00:06:47,700
take a breath, take a step, sit down with me.

112
00:06:47,700 --> 00:06:53,460
I'm not going to take all day, you know, I know they don't have all day, but...

113
00:06:53,700 --> 00:07:00,540
I think we're going to at least get somewhere out of this interaction rather than

114
00:07:01,740 --> 00:07:06,380
nowhere or somewhere negative more often than not.

